T(31, "31-5", "Crows That Make Tools", "Information Report", [
"On the island of New Caledonia, in the South Pacific, lives a bird that has puzzled and impressed scientists for decades. New Caledonian crows are glossy, black birds with heavy, straight beaks. Their forest home is full of dead wood, which is exactly where their favourite food hides. At first glance, there is nothing remarkable about them. Yet they do something that very few animals in the world can do: they make tools.",
"The crows\u2019 favourite food is a fat grub that lives in holes in rotting wood, well out of reach of a beak. To get at it, a crow chooses a twig, snaps it to the right length and trims off any leaves and side shoots. It then pushes the twig into the hole, waits for the grub to bite the end, and draws its meal out. It is delicate work that demands patience. Some crows go even further. They snap a twig from a fork, leaving a small hook at the tip, and they shape it carefully with their beaks. Others tear strips from the stiff, spiky leaves of a plant called pandanus, producing narrow tools with a jagged edge.",
"Crows appear to value their tools. They have been seen carrying a favourite tool from one feeding site to the next rather than making a new one each time. This behaviour is unusual, because it suggests that the crows are planning ahead and treating the tool as something worth keeping.",
"Young crows are not born knowing how to make tools. They stay close to their parents for many months, watching closely as the adults work. Young birds who watch adults using tools can be seen handling twigs themselves, and it takes a long time before they are skilful. In other words, the skill is learned, not simply inherited.",
"One of the most famous experiments involved a crow at a university in Oxford. The researchers placed a tiny bucket of food at the bottom of a narrow, upright tube, and gave the bird a straight piece of wire. A straight wire was useless for lifting the bucket. The crow wedged one end of the wire into a gap, pulled the other end with its beak until it bent into a hook, and then used it to lift the bucket out. No crow in the wild uses wire, so the bird had solved a brand-new problem rather than repeating something it already knew.",
"Scientists still argue about how much crows really understand. Some think the birds reason about cause and effect, while others believe they rely on trial and error and good memory. Whichever explanation is correct, the crows have shown that a small brain in a feathered head can do remarkably clever things. Tool use is not, it seems, a skill reserved for humans and their closest relatives.",
], [
 dict(d="2b",k="mc",m=1,q="Where do New Caledonian crows live?",opts=["On an island in the South Pacific","In the deserts of Africa","In the forests of northern Europe","On mountains in Asia"],a="A"),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 2, what does the word \u201cjagged\u201d mean?",opts=["Very smooth","Rough and uneven","Curved like a hook","Extremely long"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the word from paragraph 1 that tells you what colour the crows are.",a="black",g="Accept \u201cblack\u201d only. (Do not accept \u201cglossy\u201d.)"),
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="What do the crows use their twig tools to reach?",a="A grub hiding in a hole in rotting wood.",g="Accept grub(s). Do not accept \u201cinsects\u201d unless \u201cgrub\u201d is also mentioned."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each sentence.",items=["Crows sometimes make hooks from twigs.","Young crows are born knowing how to make tools.","The wire experiment took place in Oxford.","Scientists all agree about how crows think."],a=["T","F","T","F"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the steps from 1 to 4 to show how a crow uses a twig to catch a grub.",items=["The crow draws out its meal.","The crow snaps a twig to the right length.","The grub bites the end of the twig.","The crow pushes the twig into the hole."],a=[4,1,3,2],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="How do we know that crows learn to make tools rather than being born knowing how? Give two pieces of evidence.",a="They stay close to their parents for many months watching them; young birds handle twigs themselves; it takes a long time before they are skilful.",g="1 mark each for two different points from paragraph 4. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why does the author say that carrying a favourite tool from place to place is unusual?",a="It suggests the crows plan ahead and treat the tool as valuable.",g="Accept any answer about planning ahead or valuing the tool."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="In the wire experiment, why was it important that no crow in the wild uses wire?",a="It showed the crow was solving a new problem rather than repeating something it already knew.",g="Accept any answer that says the crow could not have learned the trick before / it had to work it out."),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=1,q="The author says \u201ca small brain in a feathered head can do remarkably clever things\u201d. What effect does this have on the reader?",a="It makes us feel surprised and impressed by how clever the crows are.",g="Accept any answer about surprise, admiration or challenging what we expect of small animals."),
 dict(d="2a",k="short",m=1,q="In paragraph 6, what does the word \u201creserved\u201d mean in \u201ca skill reserved for humans\u201d?",a="Kept for / only for.",g="Accept: set aside for, limited to, belonging only to."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which sentence best sums up the whole text?",opts=["Crows are the cleverest animals in the world.","Crows in New Caledonia can make and use tools, which shows how clever they are.","Grubs are hard to find in wood.","Scientists cannot explain anything about crows."],a="B"),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these is the best heading for paragraph 4?",opts=["Learning a skill by watching","Food in the wild","Crows and wire","Where crows nest"],a="A"),
 dict(d="2e",k="short",m=1,q="If the crow in the experiment had been given a piece of string, what do you think it would have done? Use what you have read to help you.",a="It would probably have tried to make it into a tool / used it to hook the bucket.",g="Accept any sensible prediction that shows the crow solving the problem with the object."),
])

T(31, "31-6", "The Coat from the Charity Shop", "Narrative", [
"The coat had been on the rail for three weeks before Ines finally bought it. It was long and green, its sleeves worn thin at the elbows, and it smelt faintly of lavender and old paper. \u201cFour pounds,\u201d said the woman at the till, without looking up from her crossword. \u201cNobody else wanted it.\u201d Ines, who was saving for a new pair of trainers and could not really afford anything, paid up anyway. She could not have said why.",
"It was on the bus home that she felt something hard in the lining, near the hem. Pushing her fingers through a small tear, she drew out a brass key, no longer than her thumb, tied with a scrap of blue ribbon. Attached to it was a card, its handwriting so old-fashioned that she had to tilt it towards the window to read it. \u201cIf you have found this,\u201d it said, \u201cask at number 9, Coldharbour Lane.\u201d",
"Ines read it four times. Then she got off the bus two stops early. Coldharbour Lane was a narrow street of tall terraced houses, their doors painted in faded shades of red and green and blue. Number 9 was the last of them, with a crooked gate and a window curtained in lace. Ines stood on the pavement for a long minute, her mind racing through every book she had ever read: buried treasure, a secret room, a missing will.",
"The door was opened by a small, upright woman with silver hair and a pencil tucked behind one ear. She looked at the coat, then at the key in Ines\u2019s hand, and her whole face relaxed into a smile. \u201cWell,\u201d she said. \u201cThree weeks. I was beginning to think nobody was curious any more.\u201d",
"Ines stared. She had seen that pencil before, and the way the woman held her head slightly to one side. \u201cYou were at the till,\u201d she said slowly. \u201cYou said nobody else wanted it.\u201d \u201cNobody else did,\u201d said the woman. \u201cThat is rather the point.\u201d",
"She led Ines down a narrow hall and into a front room where the curtains were half drawn. In the corner, beneath a knitted shawl, stood an upright piano, its lid firmly shut. \u201cMy name is Mrs Bhatt,\u201d she said, \u201cand I taught music in this street for forty years. My fingers no longer do as they are told, and the piano has been silent since the spring. It is a poor thing for an instrument to be locked up with nobody to play it.\u201d She nodded at the key. \u201cI hoped that someone might come and turn it.\u201d",
"\u201cSo the coat was a trick,\u201d said Ines. \u201cAn invitation,\u201d Mrs Bhatt corrected her, gently. \u201cA trick suggests I meant to fool you.\u201d",
"Ines looked at the key, then at the piano, then back at the key. She thought of the trainers she had been saving for, and found that they mattered a little less than they had that morning. She knelt, fitted the key into the lock and turned it. The lid rose with a sigh, and the first note she pressed rang out through the room, low and true, as if the piano had been waiting for her all along.",
], [
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 3, what does the word \u201cracing\u201d suggest about Ines\u2019s thoughts?",opts=["They were fast and full of ideas.","They were slow and calm.","They were dull and bored.","They were scared and sad."],a="A"),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the word from paragraph 2 that tells you what colour the ribbon was.",a="blue",g="Accept \u201cblue\u201d only."),
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="Where did the card tell Ines to go?",a="Number 9, Coldharbour Lane.",g="Accept: number 9 / 9 Coldharbour Lane."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each sentence.",items=["Ines paid four pounds for the coat.","The key was found in one of the coat\u2019s pockets.","The woman at the till was doing a crossword.","Mrs Bhatt had taught music for forty years."],a=["T","F","T","T"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the events from 1 to 4 to show the order they happened in.",items=["Ines found a key in the lining.","Mrs Bhatt explained why she had left the key.","Ines bought the coat.","Ines stood outside number 9."],a=[3,1,4,2],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="What clues in paragraph 5 tell Ines that Mrs Bhatt was the woman at the till? Give one.",a="The pencil / the way she held her head to one side.",g="Accept either clue."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Why did Mrs Bhatt want someone to find the key? Give two reasons from the text.",a="Her fingers no longer work well enough to play; the piano has been silent since the spring; she thinks it is sad for an instrument to be locked up with nobody to play it; she hoped someone would come to turn the key.",g="1 mark each for two different reasons. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2d",k="mc",m=1,q="What does Mrs Bhatt mean when she says \u201cNobody else did. That is rather the point\u201d?",opts=["She wanted the coat to be cheap.","She wanted to hide the key from the customers.","She was angry that no one bought the coat.","She was sure the coat would be found by someone curious, because nobody else wanted it."],a="D"),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=1,q="Mrs Bhatt corrects Ines: \u201cAn invitation. A trick suggests I meant to fool you.\u201d Why does she choose the word \u201cinvitation\u201d?",a="Because she meant to welcome Ines, not to deceive her.",g="Accept any answer about kindness or welcome rather than deceiving."),
 dict(d="2a",k="short",m=1,q="In paragraph 6, the piano is described as \u201csilent\u201d. What does \u201csilent\u201d mean?",a="Making no sound / not played.",g="Accept: quiet, without sound."),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=2,q="The last sentence says the note rang out \u201cas if the piano had been waiting for her all along\u201d. What does this make you think about the piano and about Ines? Give two ideas.",a="That the piano was lonely and had been waiting for a player; that Ines was the right person to play it.",g="1 mark for the piano being waiting or lonely. 1 mark for Ines being the right person / it being meant to be."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which sentence best sums up the story?",opts=["A girl buys a coat and finds a treasure hidden in its lining.","A girl follows a note hidden in a coat and finds that a lonely piano teacher wants someone to play her piano.","A girl gets lost on her way to buy trainers.","A shop assistant tricks a girl into paying too much."],a="B"),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="What does the sentence \u201cShe thought of the trainers she had been saving for, and found that they mattered a little less\u201d tell you about how Ines is feeling?",a="That she is more interested in the piano and the moment than in buying things.",g="Accept: she is caught up in something more special / has found something more valuable."),
 dict(d="2e",k="short",m=1,q="What do you think Ines and Mrs Bhatt might do after this? Give a reason.",a="Ines will learn or play the piano with Mrs Bhatt as her teacher, because Mrs Bhatt wanted someone to play it.",g="Accept any sensible prediction based on the ending."),
])
